## Overview

| Version | Target platform | Release date (planned) | Major new capabilities |
|---------|-----------------|------------------------|------------------------|
| **C# 12** | .NET 8 (preview 1/2) | **Nov 2023** | • Primary constructors<br>• Enhanced pattern‑matching (and/or/not, relational patterns)<br>• Record‑type and record‑struct improvements (inheritance, `with`‑expressions)<br>• Static‑abstract member extensions (generic math)<br>• Other small syntax & performance improvements |
| **C# 13** | .NET 9 (preview 1/2) | **Nov 2024** | • Global attributes (apply an attribute to an entire assembly or namespace)<br>• Required‑member defaults and improved `init` handling<br>• Record‑struct inheritance and constructor forwarding<br>• Pattern‑matching refinements (list‑patterns, relational patterns, `and`/`or`/`not` patterns)<br>• More powerful interpolated‑string handlers<br>• Static‑abstract property initializers & other generic‑math polish |

> **Tip**: All of the features above are available in the *preview* releases of .NET 8/9.  The final versions may tweak the wording, add additional syntax, or change the release date.

---

## C# 12 – The “Big Bang” of C# 12

| Feature | What it adds | Why it matters |
|---------|--------------|----------------|
| **Primary Constructors** | Allows a type to declare its constructor parameters inline in the type declaration. | Removes boilerplate `ctor` definitions for simple data types, e.g. `class Person(string FirstName, string LastName);` |
| **Record‑type & Record‑struct Enhancements** | • Record structs can now inherit from other record structs.<br>• The `with` expression works on record structs and can target non‑init fields.<br>• Positional record constructors can be used with generic types. | Enables fully immutable value types that can participate in the same inheritance hierarchy as classes. |
| **Pattern‑matching Power‑Ups** | • `and`, `or`, and `not` patterns are added.<br>• Relational patterns (`>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`) now support `int`, `double`, `decimal`, `DateTime`, `TimeSpan`, and user‑defined types via static abstract operators.<br>• List patterns get a `..` element to capture “rest” items. | Lets you express complex conditions in a single, readable `switch` or `if`. |
| **Static‑abstract Member Extensions** | • Static abstract members can now be declared for properties, indexers, and events.<br>• `Math` generic functions gain `operator +, -, *, /, %` and `Math`‑like static abstract methods. | Makes generic math and algebraic APIs far more expressive. |
| **`global using` Enhancements** | `global using` can now reference aliases and nested namespaces more cleanly; it also works in *conditional* files. | Keeps your codebase tidy by moving ubiquitous `using` statements to a single spot. |
| **`required` Member Extensions** | Fields can now be marked `required` (in addition to properties). | Helps designers enforce that all members are initialized without having to write a constructor. |
| **Miscellaneous** | • `default!` suppression syntax for nullable contexts.<br>• New language “target‑typed” `new` improvements for `unmanaged` constraints.<br>• Some compiler‑level performance tweaks. | Minor but useful for smoother coding and faster builds. |

### Example: Primary Constructor + Pattern Matching

```csharp
// Primary constructor: concise data holder
public record Customer(string FirstName, string LastName)
{
    // Secondary constructor for optional middle name
    public Customer(string first, string middle, string last) : this(first, last)
    {
        MiddleName = middle;
    }

    public string? MiddleName { get; init; }
}

// Pattern matching in a switch
static void PrintCustomerInfo(Customer c) => c switch
{
    // Match a customer whose first name starts with 'J' AND last name ends with 'son'
    var (first, _, last) when first.StartsWith("J") && last.EndsWith("son") => 
        Console.WriteLine($"{first} {last} (J‑prefix)"),

    // Use `not` pattern to exclude a known name
    not { FirstName: "Alice", LastName: "Smith" } => Console.WriteLine("Unknown customer"),

    // Default
    _ => Console.WriteLine("Customer")
};
```

